\section{Related Work}
\label{sec:related_work}

\paragraph{Ambiguity in NLP and requirements engineering.}
Ambiguity has been studied extensively in both computational linguistics and software engineering. \citet{li_ambiguity_taxonomy_2024} propose an 11-type NLP taxonomy covering syntactic, semantic, and pragmatic ambiguity, from which we draw our linguistic-layer types. In requirements engineering (RE), \citet{massey_ambiguity_re_2014} classify six ambiguity types in regulatory texts, and \citet{berry_kamsties_2004} introduce the distinction between nocuous ambiguity (where stakeholders diverge on interpretation) and innocuous ambiguity (where they converge). Computational detection of nocuous ambiguity has progressed from heuristic methods \citep{chantree_2006,willis_nocuous_2008} through NLP-based cross-domain detectors \citep{ferrari_ambiguity_2019,ferrari_defects_2018} to transformer-based approaches for anaphoric ambiguity \citep{ezzini_taphsir_2022,ezzini_anaphoric_2022}. \citet{zhao_nlp4re_2021} survey the broader NLP4RE landscape. Our work extends Berry and Kamsties' framework to LLM agents, where the absence of shared organizational context makes previously innocuous ambiguity nocuous.

\paragraph{Psycholinguistics of ambiguity processing.}
\citet{ferreira_good_enough_2002} show that human comprehenders build shallow, ``good-enough'' representations rather than full parses, a finding that parallels our observation that LLM agents adopt surface interpretations of ambiguous policy clauses. \citet{frazier_rayner_1982} demonstrate that readers commit to a single syntactic parse early and backtrack upon disambiguation failure, while \citet{levy_expectation_2008} formalizes this as expectation-based processing. \citet{li_garden_path_llm_2024} provide direct evidence that LLMs exhibit human-like garden-path effects, and \citet{poesio_artstein_2005} formalize genuine ambiguity in anaphoric annotation as inherent rather than resolvable. \citet{lassiter_goodman_2017} model vague adjective interpretation in a Bayesian framework. \citet{liu_ambiguity_2023} demonstrate that language models generally fail to represent ambiguity, collapsing multiple valid interpretations into a single output. These findings ground our ``Ambiguity Blindness'' result: agents, like humans, process ambiguous input without meta-cognitive awareness, but unlike humans, they lack the social and organizational channels to recover.

\paragraph{Agent safety evaluation.}
The agent safety evaluation landscape includes benchmarks for risk awareness \citep{rjudge_2024}, tool-use safety \citep{toolemu_2024}, indirect prompt injection \citep{injecagent_2024}, and policy-invisible violations \citep{phantompolicy_2026}. \citet{shieldagent_2025} extract formal safety rules from policy documents, and AgentAuditor \citep{agentauditor_2025} achieves human-level safety evaluation. Our work differs by focusing on the \emph{source specification} rather than the agent's execution: we measure how ambiguity in the policy itself causes safety violations, complementing work that assumes clean policies.

\paragraph{Policy specification for agents.}
DRIP-R \citep{drip_r_2026} is the closest concurrent work: it benchmarks 400 policy-ambiguous retail scenarios across five RE ambiguity types, measuring decision quality rather than safety violations. Our work differs in two respects: (1) our matched-pair design establishes causality rather than correlating ambiguity presence with decision quality; (2) we quantify the model$\times$type interaction, revealing that authorization scope serves as a model-differentiating canary type. PolicyBank \citep{policybank_2026} studies iterative policy refinement, closing 82\% of the gap to a human oracle. AgentPex \citep{agentpex_2026} detects procedural violations in agentic traces, finding that 83\% of perfect-outcome traces contain violations. MANTRA \citep{mantra_2026} synthesizes SMT-validated compliance benchmarks from procedural manuals. $\tau$-bench \citep{tau_bench_2024} and its successor $\tau^2$-bench \citep{tau2_bench_2025} provide the conversational agent evaluation platforms.

\section{Results}
\label{sec:results}

We organize results around four analyses of increasing specificity: the binary ambiguity effect (C1), the type hierarchy (C2), the aggregate layer effect (C3), and the model$\times$type interaction (C4). C1 and C2 constitute our primary claims; C3 is inconclusive; C4 is exploratory but provides the model$\times$type interaction analysis.

\subsection{Binary Ambiguity Effect (C1)}

Across all five models and six types, ambiguous clauses increase violation rates by 34.0 pp (41.0\% vs.\ 6.9\%, Mantel--Haenszel OR $= 15.1$, 95\% CI $[10.78, 21.17]$, $p < 10^{-105}$; bootstrap 95\% CI for $\Delta$: $[31.2, 36.9]$ pp). Of 1,497 matched pairs, the discordant ratio is 15.1:1 (544 ambiguous-only vs.\ 36 unambiguous-only violations). All six types individually produce significant increases (all $p < 10^{-5}$), and all five models show large effects ($+25.7$ to $+39.0$ pp, all $p < 10^{-17}$). The perturbation control ($n = 120$) yields $\Delta_{\text{perturb}} = 3.33$ pp (95\% CI $[-5.0, +13.33]$, containing zero), confirming specificity to semantic ambiguity. Results replicate under an alternative severity threshold ($\Delta = 38.2$ pp; Appendix~\ref{app:severity}). Clause identity explains 22.4\% of variance versus 1.0\% for model identity.

\subsection{Type Hierarchy (C2)}

\begin{table*}[t]
\centering
\caption{Violation rates (\%) under ambiguous policy clauses by type and model. $\Delta$pp denotes the pooled difference between ambiguous and unambiguous conditions across all five models. All per-type differences are significant ($p < 10^{-5}$, Fisher's exact test).}
\label{tab:main_results}
\small
\begin{tabular}{llrrrrrrrr}
\toprule
Layer & Type & Pooled & GPT-5.4 & GPT-4.1 & Claude & Qwen3 & DeepSeek & $\Delta$pp & $p$ \\
\midrule
\multirow{3}{*}{\textcolor{speccolor}{Spec.}} & Incompleteness & 53.6 & 46.0 & 58.0 & 48.0 & 62.0 & 54.0 & +49.2 & $<10^{-37}$ \\
 & Auth.\ Scope & 41.6 & 64.0 & 46.0 & 12.0 & 26.0 & 60.0 & +36.0 & $<10^{-22}$ \\
 & Cond.\ Precedence & 36.8 & 34.0 & 36.0 & 36.0 & 40.0 & 38.0 & +30.8 & $<10^{-17}$ \\
\midrule
\multirow{3}{*}{\textcolor{lingcolor}{Ling.}} & Lexical & 42.8 & 42.0 & 34.0 & 32.0 & 52.0 & 54.0 & +35.2 & $<10^{-20}$ \\
 & Scopal & 39.4 & 42.0 & 38.0 & 32.0 & 36.7 & 48.0 & +34.2 & $<10^{-21}$ \\
 & Coreferential & 31.6 & 18.0 & 32.0 & 22.0 & 42.0 & 44.0 & +18.7 & $<10^{-6}$ \\
\midrule
\multicolumn{2}{l}{\textbf{Overall}} & \textbf{41.0} & 41.0 & 40.7 & 30.3 & 43.1 & 49.7 & \textbf{+34.0} & $<10^{-105}$ \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table*}

The six types are not interchangeable. An omnibus $\chi^2$ test on ambiguous-condition violation rates rejects the uniformity null ($\chi^2 = 28.02$, $df = 5$, $p = 3.6 \times 10^{-5}$, Cram\'{e}r's $V = 0.137$; statistical power $= 0.993$ at the observed effect). TOST equivalence testing at a $\pm 15$ pp margin reveals a three-tier structure: incompleteness stands alone at the top (53.6\%), a mid-tier cluster spans four types (lexical 42.8\%, authorization scope 41.6\%, scopal 39.4\%, conditional precedence 36.8\%), and coreferential occupies the bottom (31.6\%). Table~\ref{tab:main_results} and Figure~\ref{fig:forest_plot} present the full breakdown.

Sensitivity analysis across seven margins ($\pm 5$--$20$ pp; Appendix~\ref{app:tost}) confirms this structure.

Incompleteness produces the highest violation rate in four of five models (all except GPT-5.4, where authorization scope ranks first at 64\%). Incompleteness is structurally distinct: its unambiguous variant necessarily adds policy content rather than merely resolving an existing ambiguity. We retain it because missing policy information is the most ecologically prevalent form of underspecification, but its 49.2 pp effect should be interpreted as an upper bound. A logistic regression controlling for per-clause information delta confirms incompleteness remains individually significant ($p = 0.022$, coefficient change $< 8\%$), while coreferential, whose variants are lexically identical (info delta $= 0$), provides a confound-free lower bound at 31.6\%. Excluding incompleteness, the remaining five types show a non-significant omnibus difference ($p = 0.079$), but pairwise contrasts reveal a two-tier pattern: authorization scope ($p_{\text{adj}} = 0.039$) and lexical ($p_{\text{adj}} = 0.025$) remain significantly above coreferential.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig2_forest_plot.pdf}
\caption{Per-type effect sizes ($\Delta$pp, ambiguous minus unambiguous) with Newcombe 95\% CIs. All types produce significant increases. A three-tier hierarchy emerges: incompleteness $\gg$ mid cluster $\gg$ coreferential.}
\label{fig:forest_plot}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Layer Effect (C3)}

Specification-layer types trend higher than linguistic-layer types (44.0\% vs.\ 37.9\%, $\Delta = 6.08$ pp, $p = 0.017$, $V = 0.062$), but C3 is inconclusive: it does not survive Holm--Bonferroni correction, neither within-judge subgroup reaches significance individually, and a logistic regression adding per-clause token delta flips the coefficient sign ($\beta = -0.10$, $p = 0.384$). The layer gap varies from $+14.0$ pp (GPT-5.4) to $-0.9$ pp (Qwen3), with cross-layer type overlap (conditional precedence 36.8\% vs.\ scopal 39.4\%). We treat C3 as inconclusive.

\subsection{Model $\times$ Type Interaction (C4) \emph{[Exploratory]}}
\label{sec:c4}

\begin{figure*}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{figures/fig3_heatmap.pdf}
\caption{Violation rates (\%) by model and ambiguity type under ambiguous conditions ($n = 2{,}997$). Authorization scope varies 52 pp across models (Claude 12\% to GPT-5.4 64\%), while incompleteness remains uniformly high (46--62\%).}
\label{fig:heatmap}
\end{figure*}

A model main effect is significant ($\chi^2 = 31.02$, $p = 3.0 \times 10^{-6}$, $V = 0.102$), with Claude (30.3\%) substantially less vulnerable than DeepSeek (49.7\%). Model identity explains only 1.0\% of total violation variance; the significant interaction ($F(20) = 1.97$, $p = 0.006$; 2.9\% of variance) concentrates in specific type--model combinations. GPT-5.4 ($V = 0.28$, $p < 0.001$) and Claude ($V = 0.24$, $p = 0.003$) show the strongest type differentiation, while DeepSeek ($V = 0.14$, $p = 0.28$) shows no significant type effect.

Figure~\ref{fig:heatmap} highlights three patterns: (1) authorization scope is the \emph{model-differentiating type} (52 pp range; Claude 12\% to GPT-5.4 64\%), consistent with safety training that addresses boundary reasoning; (2) incompleteness is a \emph{universal vulnerability} (46--62\%, 16 pp range); (3) conditional precedence is the most \emph{model-invariant type} (34--40\%, 6 pp range). The absolute authorization scope spread may partly reflect judge severity calibration (\S\ref{sec:method}); the qualitative pattern (Claude lowest, DeepSeek highest) is robust under both binary and moderate-plus thresholds.

The authorization scope interaction survives within-judge analysis: Claude, Qwen3, and DeepSeek (all judged by GPT-5.4) show rates of 12\%, 26\%, and 60\% respectively, ruling out judge confound.

\section{Error Analysis}
\label{sec:analysis}

The following failure mode taxonomy emerges empirically from the coded traces, distinct from the input ambiguity taxonomy (\S\ref{sec:taxonomy}).

We analyze 614 violation traces across all five models, coded into five failure modes: unauthorized escalation, assumption-based action, scope misapplication, arbitrary rule selection, and conservative refusal. GPT-5.4 and GPT-4.1 serve as detailed annotation anchors because they span the judge boundary. Inter-coder reliability is substantial (Gwet's AC$_1$ $= 0.62$ \citep{gwet_2014}; Cohen's $\kappa = 0.52$ \citep{cohen_1960}; the gap reflects the prevalence paradox, as three categories account for ${\sim}$90\% of instances).

\subsection{Type-Specific Failure Mechanisms}

The five-model data reveal three mechanistically distinct failure patterns that crosscut the specification/linguistic layer boundary. \emph{Structural misparse} (scope misapplication): the agent constructs an incorrect parse and follows it consistently, paralleling human garden-path processing \citep{frazier_rayner_1982} and the good-enough framework \citep{ferreira_good_enough_2002}. This dominates scopal (all 5 models), conditional precedence (all 5), and coreferential (3/5). \emph{Information gap-filling} (assumption-based action): the agent fills missing or ambiguous information with plausible but incorrect defaults. This dominates incompleteness (3/5) and lexical (3/5). \emph{Boundary violation} (unauthorized escalation): the agent exceeds permission boundaries when authorization scope is underspecified. This dominates authorization scope exclusively (all 5 models).

Each type's dominant failure mode is majority-consistent ($\geq$3/5 models agree), with three types achieving full unanimity (authorization scope, conditional precedence, scopal). Per-model validation on GPT-5.4 and GPT-4.1 confirms five of six types share the same dominant mode across both models. The layer distinction does not hold at the mechanistic level: conditional precedence (specification-layer) shares its dominant mode with scopal (linguistic-layer), while authorization scope follows a distinct boundary-violation pattern absent from other specification-layer types.

\subsection{Ambiguity Blindness}

Agents rarely recognize ambiguity. We code \emph{disambiguation awareness} (acknowledging multiple interpretations, asking clarifying questions, or conditioning on ambiguity) versus \emph{surface adoption} (proceeding with a single interpretation). Overall disambiguation awareness is 4.6\% across all five models: Claude (11.0\%) and GPT-4.1 (10.7\%) show the highest rates, GPT-5.4 shows 4.1\%, while Qwen3 and DeepSeek show zero disambiguation awareness across all 129 and 149 violation traces respectively. Surface adoption exceeds 88\% for every type, with user escalation below 1\%. We term this \emph{Ambiguity Blindness}: agents process ambiguous clauses without meta-cognitive awareness, adopting surface interpretations by default, consistent with \citet{hu_ambiguity_2025}'s finding that ambiguity in LLMs reflects missing conceptual representations.

\subsection{Systematic Nocuity}

Among ambiguous clauses, 41.3\% trigger majority-convergent violations ($\geq$3/5 models), with 13\% producing universal violations. This extends \citet{berry_kamsties_2004}'s framework with \emph{systematic nocuity}: all models converge on the same incorrect interpretation, invisible in agreement-based evaluation. Incompleteness has the highest universal convergence (24\%).

