A model main effect is significant ($\chi^2 = 31.02$, $p = 3.0 \times 10^{-6}$, $V = 0.102$), with Claude (30.3\%) substantially less vulnerable than DeepSeek (49.7\%). Model identity explains only 1.0\% of total violation variance; the significant interaction ($F(20) = 1.97$, $p = 0.006$; 2.9\% of variance) concentrates in specific type--model combinations. GPT-5.4 ($V = 0.28$, $p < 0.001$) and Claude ($V = 0.24$, $p = 0.003$) show the strongest type differentiation, while DeepSeek ($V = 0.14$, $p = 0.28$) shows no significant type effect.

Figure~\ref{fig:heatmap} highlights three patterns: (1) authorization scope is the \emph{model-differentiating type} (52 pp range; Claude 12\% to GPT-5.4 64\%), consistent with safety training that addresses boundary reasoning; (2) incompleteness is a \emph{universal vulnerability} (46--62\%, 16 pp range); (3) conditional precedence is the most \emph{model-invariant type} (34--40\%, 6 pp range). The absolute authorization scope spread may partly reflect judge severity calibration (\S\ref{sec:method}); the qualitative pattern (Claude lowest, DeepSeek highest) is robust under both binary and moderate-plus thresholds.

The authorization scope interaction survives within-judge analysis: Claude, Qwen3, and DeepSeek (all judged by GPT-5.4) show rates of 12\%, 26\%, and 60\% respectively, ruling out judge confound.

\section{Error Analysis}
\label{sec:analysis}

The following failure mode taxonomy emerges empirically from the coded traces, distinct from the input ambiguity taxonomy (\S\ref{sec:taxonomy}).

We analyze 614 violation traces across all five models, coded into five failure modes: unauthorized escalation, assumption-based action, scope misapplication, arbitrary rule selection, and conservative refusal. GPT-5.4 and GPT-4.1 serve as detailed annotation anchors because they span the judge boundary. Inter-coder reliability is substantial (Gwet's AC$_1$ $= 0.62$ \citep{gwet_2014}; Cohen's $\kappa = 0.52$ \citep{cohen_1960}; the gap reflects the prevalence paradox, as three categories account for ${\sim}$90\% of instances).

\subsection{Type-Specific Failure Mechanisms}

The five-model data reveal three mechanistically distinct failure patterns that crosscut the specification/linguistic layer boundary. \emph{Structural misparse} (scope misapplication): the agent constructs an incorrect parse and follows it consistently, paralleling human garden-path processing \citep{frazier_rayner_1982} and the good-enough framework \citep{ferreira_good_enough_2002}. This dominates scopal (all 5 models), conditional precedence (all 5), and coreferential (3/5). \emph{Information gap-filling} (assumption-based action): the agent fills missing or ambiguous information with plausible but incorrect defaults. This dominates incompleteness (3/5) and lexical (3/5). \emph{Boundary violation} (unauthorized escalation): the agent exceeds permission boundaries when authorization scope is underspecified. This dominates authorization scope exclusively (all 5 models).

Each type's dominant failure mode is majority-consistent ($\geq$3/5 models agree), with three types achieving full unanimity (authorization scope, conditional precedence, scopal). Per-model validation on GPT-5.4 and GPT-4.1 confirms five of six types share the same dominant mode across both models. The layer distinction does not hold at the mechanistic level: conditional precedence (specification-layer) shares its dominant mode with scopal (linguistic-layer), while authorization scope follows a distinct boundary-violation pattern absent from other specification-layer types.

\subsection{Ambiguity Blindness}

Agents rarely recognize ambiguity. We code \emph{disambiguation awareness} (acknowledging multiple interpretations, asking clarifying questions, or conditioning on ambiguity) versus \emph{surface adoption} (proceeding with a single interpretation). Overall disambiguation awareness is 4.6\% across all five models: Claude (11.0\%) and GPT-4.1 (10.7\%) show the highest rates, GPT-5.4 shows 4.1\%, while Qwen3 and DeepSeek show zero disambiguation awareness across all 129 and 149 violation traces respectively. Surface adoption exceeds 88\% for every type, with user escalation below 1\%. We term this \emph{Ambiguity Blindness}: agents process ambiguous clauses without meta-cognitive awareness, adopting surface interpretations by default, consistent with \citet{hu_ambiguity_2025}'s finding that ambiguity in LLMs reflects missing conceptual representations.

\subsection{Systematic Nocuity}

Among ambiguous clauses, 41.3\% trigger majority-convergent violations ($\geq$3/5 models), with 13\% producing universal violations. This extends \citet{berry_kamsties_2004}'s framework with \emph{systematic nocuity}: all models converge on the same incorrect interpretation, invisible in agreement-based evaluation. Incompleteness has the highest universal convergence (24\%).

\section{Method}
\label{sec:method}

\subsection{Experimental Platform}

We conduct all experiments on the original release of $\tau^2$-bench \citep{tau2_bench_2025}, a dual-control conversational agent benchmark spanning retail and airline customer service domains. $\tau^2$-bench provides natural-language policy documents, tool APIs, and multi-turn user interactions with ground-truth evaluation via assertion checking. Its policy injection mechanism allows us to replace individual policy clauses while preserving the surrounding context, enabling controlled ambiguity manipulation.

\subsection{Matched-Pair Design}

For each of the six ambiguity types, we construct 50 \emph{clause pairs}: an ambiguous variant and a matched unambiguous variant that conveys the same intended policy but resolves the ambiguity. Pairs are minimal edits: the unambiguous variant adds only the information necessary to eliminate the target ambiguity (e.g., for scopal ambiguity, replacing ``items may be returned within 30 days'' with ``standard items may be returned within 30 days; customized items are final sale''). This yields 300 clause pairs $\times$ 2 conditions $\times$ 5 models $= 2{,}997$ episodes (3 episodes lost to API errors in one Qwen3 batch).

Clause pairs are generated through a template-based pipeline and reviewed by two independent annotators (both with NLP background). Each annotator independently verified the three quality criteria; rejected or modified pairs were iteratively revised until both annotators confirmed compliance. For each type, we identify policy clauses in $\tau^2$-bench that are naturally susceptible to the target ambiguity, then construct the ambiguous and unambiguous variants. All pairs are reviewed to ensure (1) the ambiguous variant contains exactly one instance of the target ambiguity type, (2) the unambiguous variant eliminates that ambiguity without introducing new information that changes the policy's scope, and (3) the ambiguity is plausibly natural (would occur in real policy documents). A post-hoc realism validation confirms that ambiguous clauses are linguistically consistent with original $\tau^2$-bench policy text (\S\ref{sec:limitations}).

\subsection{Models}

We evaluate five models spanning three providers and two architecture families: GPT-5.4 and GPT-4.1 (OpenAI, proprietary dense), Claude Sonnet 4.6 (Anthropic, proprietary dense), Qwen3-235B (Alibaba, open-source Mixture-of-Experts), and DeepSeek-V3 (DeepSeek, open-source dense). This selection covers frontier and mid-tier proprietary models alongside open-source alternatives with different training philosophies. All API calls were made on May 20, 2026.

\subsection{Evaluation}

We use a cross-judge design to mitigate self-evaluation bias: GPT-5.4 judges Claude, Qwen3, and DeepSeek traces; GPT-4.1 judges GPT-5.4 traces. We acknowledge this creates an asymmetry (a mid-tier model judging a frontier model in one direction), which we discuss in \S\ref{sec:limitations}. Judges classify each episode outcome on a four-tier severity scale: none, minor, moderate, and critical. Our primary analysis uses moderate-or-above severity as the violation threshold; minor-only deviations (6.6\% of episodes) are excluded. Including minor violations (overall rate 49.6\% vs.\ 41.0\%) produces even larger effects without changing any directional conclusion.

\paragraph{Cross-judge calibration.} To quantify potential judge bias, we conducted a dual-judge calibration on 100 stratified episodes (60 from GPT-5.4, 40 from other models; 65\% ambiguous). Both judges evaluated each episode independently. McNemar's test reveals no systematic detection bias ($p = 0.606$; GPT-4.1-only violations: 9, GPT-5.4-only: 6). Binary agreement is substantial ($\kappa = 0.653$), with weighted four-tier severity agreement reaching $\kappa_w = 0.775$. Per-type agreement is highest for incompleteness ($\kappa = 0.875$, near-perfect) and lowest for authorization scope ($\kappa = 0.385$, fair), where severity grading diverges despite binary detection agreement. Since our primary analyses use binary violation thresholds, the severity-level disagreement does not affect core conclusions (cf.\ severity sensitivity analysis, Appendix~\ref{app:severity}).

\subsection{Controls}

\paragraph{Perturbation control.} To confirm that the observed effect is specific to semantic ambiguity rather than general prompt sensitivity, we run 120 episodes with non-ambiguity perturbations: synonym substitution (replacing content words with near-synonyms) and clause reordering (shuffling the order of policy sub-clauses). The perturbation effect is $\Delta_{\text{perturb}} = 3.33$ pp (95\% CI $[-5.0, 13.3]$, containing zero), compared to the ambiguity effect of $\Delta_{\text{ambig}} = 34.0$ pp. Under the worst-case reading (CI upper bound 13.3 pp), surface perturbation accounts for at most 39\% of the observed effect, leaving a residual $\geq$20.7 pp attributable to ambiguity. A post-hoc sensitivity analysis indicates that with $n = 120$ episodes at $\alpha = 0.05$ and 80\% power, the design can detect perturbation effects $\geq 15$ pp, sufficient to rule out effects approaching the ambiguity magnitude but not small perturbation effects.

\paragraph{Reproducibility.} To verify that violation rates are stable across independent runs, we replicate 30 clause pairs (selected via stratified random sampling, 5 per type) $\times$ 2 models $\times$ 3 replicates. The intraclass correlation coefficient is ICC $= 0.919$ (95\% CI $[0.892, 0.940]$), indicating excellent reproducibility.

\subsection{Statistical Analysis}

We use McNemar's test for paired binary outcomes, $\chi^2$ tests with Cram\'{e}r's $V$ for omnibus type and model effects, Fisher's exact test for per-type comparisons, TOST equivalence testing \citep{lakens_2017}, and bootstrap 95\% CIs (10{,}000 resamples). We set the primary SESOI at $\pm 15$ pp: in safety-critical agent deployment, a type producing $\geq$15 pp more violations warrants distinct policy handling. This margin exceeds the perturbation control bound (13.3 pp CI upper bound), ensuring that equivalence conclusions are not driven by surface-level prompt variation. Sensitivity analysis across seven margins ($\pm 5$--20 pp) confirms robustness (Appendix~\ref{app:tost}). Holm--Bonferroni correction is applied within three families: (i) six per-type Fisher tests, (ii) five per-model McNemar tests, and (iii) fifteen pairwise type comparisons. C3's individual $p = 0.017$ does not survive correction within the four-claim family, consistent with its classification as inconclusive.
